    "id": "uk-to-explore-even-closer-ties-with-eu-a-9a228901",
    "title": "UK to explore even closer ties with EU after agreeing deal, says Burnham",
    "titleJa": "UK to explore even closer ties with EU after agreeing deal, says Burnham",
    "summaryJa": "The UK-EU deal agreed on Friday covers British food exports, emissions trading and a youth experience scheme.",
    "bodyOriginal": "UK to explore even closer ties with EU after agreeing deal, says Burnham\n- Published\nAndy Burnham said the UK will discuss possible options for even closer ties with the EU, after agreeing a deal covering British food exports, emissions trading and a youth experience scheme.\nThe prime minister said it was a \"good day\" for Britain and Europe, adding the Treasury believes the deal could boost the UK economy by up to £17bn a year.\nEuropean Council President António Costa said the EU remains \"open to have the closest possible relationship\" with the UK.\nConservative leader Kemi Badenoch said the deal \"isn't fair\" - and Reform's Nigel Farage called it a \"betrayal of the British people\".\nLegislation to implement the deal is expected to be introduced in the UK Parliament next week.\nBurnham arrived in Brussels to finalise the latest stage of the government's post-Brexit \"reset\" of relations with the EU, which originally began under former prime minister Sir Keir Starmer.\nThe agreements announced on Friday included scrapping most of the checks on British food exports, in return for the UK following some EU regulations, from 2028.\nThe UK government believes the changes will cut costs for businesses and could bring prices down for shoppers, while critics argue the change will make the UK a \"rule taker\" from Brussels rather than a rule maker.\nThe new youth experience scheme will allow up to 100,000 18 to 31 year olds from the EU to travel, work and study in the UK for up to three years. The same number of young Britons will be allowed into Europe.\nThe two sides want to ensure that opportunities under the annual scheme will be available to young people by no later than January 2030.\nA further six months is available to discuss a deal on tuition fees, which could prove to be a potential stumbling block.\nThere have been calls from the European side for fees to be reduced for EU students at British universities. The joint statement says any agreement would also be for UK students in the EU.\nBurnham gets his first taste of EU dealmaking and seems hungry for more\n- Published14 hours ago\nWhat is the EU-UK youth experience scheme and when will it start?\n- Published14 hours ago\nFaisal Islam: This cost-cutting UK-EU deal doesn't mean lower prices will follow\n- Published14 hours ago\nEuropean Commission President Ursula von der Leyen said she is \"confident\" a solution will be found, with Burnham adding: \"Me too.\"\nOn immigration, the UK and EU have committed to working closely to tackle migrant smuggling and human trafficking. They also expect their law enforcement agencies to share more data.\nBurnham said \"significant progress\" has been made to help \"disrupt the gangs who are making those dangerous crossings across the English Channel\".\nThe UK-EU statement also said talks on linking the EU and UK emissions trading systems have concluded and they aim to apply the agreement from 2028.\n'Brainstorming process'\nBurnham argued that polling suggests the UK public \"want closer ties\" with the EU and the deal \"delivers that\".\nHe added: \"Beyond that we do need to consider what is the right long-term relationship. Incremental steps only take us so far.\"\nThe prime minister said his government will publish a 10-year plan next month, adding it will set out its ambitions for reindustrialisation, growth in \"all parts of Britain\" and opportunities for young people.\nBurnham said these ambitions are \"connected to our long-term relationship with our nearest partner\".\nHe said over the coming months before the next UK-EU summit both sides will \"seek to conduct an informal brainstorming process\" to look at what those options are.\nBurnham added: \"What we are announcing today is probably the limit of what we can achieve in this Parliament, given what needs to be done to implement it.\n\"But we want to inform the Parliament after this one, so that decisions can be taken about that long-term relationship.\"\nLabour's 2024 general election manifesto pledged there would be \"no return to the single market, the customs union, or freedom of movement\", and Burnham has committed to this.\nThe prime minister said the \"British people will be the boss of what happens next\", with the next election needing to be held by August 2029.\nVon der Leyen, who said the summit was a \"turning point\" for the EU-UK relationship, said \"we are open for brainstorming\".\n'Endless betrayal'\nBadenoch said the deal was not fair because UK farmers would now have to follow rules made by Brussels and more young people will come to the UK to \"compete with our young people who are struggling to get jobs\".\nBadenoch was also asked about the Treasury's analysis, which Burnham said suggested the deal \"will add up to 0.5%\" to UK gross domestic product and he described it as a \"£17bn boost to the British economy\".\nShe claimed Burnham has \"plucked that figure out of the air\" and \"the only modelling I've seen that even talks about that figure says it's actually 0.0%\".\nFarage said Friday was a \"very good day for the EU\", adding: \"A good day to try and humiliate the one member state that actually had the courage to say, 'We don't need to be part of you, we can govern our own country'.\"\nIn a social media post, Farage, who was a leading figure in the 2016 campaign to get Britain out of the EU, added: \"Like every prime minister before him, Burnham is a total fraud. Only Reform will stop the endless betrayal of the British people.\"\nLiberal Democrat leader Sir Ed Davey said there needed to be more than \"tweaks\" to the UK's relationship to the EU.\n\"A youth mobility scheme is a big step forward, but it's nowhere near enough. We need a whole new deal that actually works for Britain, and fast.\"\nSign up for our Politics Essential newsletter to keep up with the inner workings of Westminster and beyond.\nRelated topics\n- Published12 November",
